# Whitelist approach: ignore everything, then explicitly allow what we publish.
# This repository must never contain Kickstart ROMs, Workbench images, original
# Transport Tycoon Deluxe data or any other licensed material.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow: the sources, what is needed to compile them, and
# enough documentation to reproduce the build. Internal working notes stay out.
/*

!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/BUILDING.md
!/LICENSE
!/COPYING
!/native/
!/build/
!/screenshots/
# The complete diff of our changes to OpenTTD's own source. GPL v2 requires the
# corresponding source for the binaries we ship, and native/ alone is not it.
!/patches/
# Generator for the EHB palette/remap tables compiled into the driver. It
# asserts the hardware half-brite rule before writing, so the tables cannot
# silently drift from what the chipset actually derives.
!/tools/gen_ehb_data.py

# Inside build/, only the scripts that actually build something.
/build/*
!/build/apply-amiga-patches.py
!/build/build-openttd.sh
# build-both.sh builds the PC targets we now ship alongside the Amiga one, from
# the same sources; without it those binaries cannot be reproduced. Its helpers
# generate the PC source list and re-apply the three gcc-11 fixes.
!/build/build-both.sh
!/build/pc-fixes.py
!/build/pc-fixes.sh
!/build/pc-source-list.sh
!/build/pc-fixup-makefile.sh
# Packs the whole release set (music tiers + PC) and refuses to ship an archive
# with backslash paths, which broke v0.9.1.
!/build/make-release-all.sh
!/build/make-release-zip.sh

# build-with-ice-retry.sh is THE build entry point - it forces the files that
# this toolchain miscompiles at -O1 down to -O0 and refuses to degrade the video
# driver. Building without it silently produces a broken game, so it has to ship.
!/build/build-with-ice-retry.sh
# build-nofpu.sh switches a configured tree to -mcpu=68020 -msoft-float, which
# is what removes the FPU requirement. It replaces build-release-softfloat.sh,
# whose -m68040 -msoft-float never actually reached the libraries.
!/build/build-nofpu.sh
!/build/tgp-remove-pow.py
!/build/make-splash.py
# Generator for the Workbench .info icon shipped with the release, built from
# the splash board art (rounded corners, no text). Produces a dual-format icon:
# a palette-safe 4-colour OldIcon for Workbench 3.1 and an OS3.5 ColorIcon.
!/build/make-info.py
# Standalone probe that proves whether printf traps on an FPU-less CPU. It logs
# through dos.library only, so the log survives even when printf itself dies.
!/build/probe/
!/build/probe/t_printf.c

# The splash screen: source image and its generator. The generated splash.dat is
# re-allowed at the end of this file - it has to come after the "*.dat" rule.
!/amiga_openttd2.png

# Compiled Amiga executables carry no extension, so match them by name.
/native/agabench
/native/agac2p
/native/againput
/native/gfxtest
/native/exctest
/native/exc_*
/native/fptest
/native/fptest_*
/native/zlibtest
/native/zsrc_*
/native/z_*
/native/paulatest

# Emulator disk images, ROMs, game data, build output
*.hdf
*.HDF
*.rom
*.ROM
*.adf
*.lha
*.grf
*.cat
*.dat
*.sav
*.obg
*.obs
*.obm
*.o
*.a
*.exe
*.dll
*.log

# Vendored CyberGraphX developer headers: "Copyright (c) 1996-1998 by phase5
# digital products", with no redistribution licence we can point to. They are
# needed to compile the RTG path, but they are not ours to publish - see
# patches/README.md for where to get them. Replacing them with our own minimal
# declarations would close this gap.
/native/openttd/cgx-include/

# Editor / OS noise
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
*~

# Kopie zapasowe robione recznie przed wieksza zmiana. Whitelist wpuszcza
# wszystko z /native/, wiec bez tego amiga_v.cpp.bak-frameskip (64 kB starej
# wersji sterownika) wjechalby do wydania i wygladal jak drugi, konkurencyjny
# plik zrodlowy.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.orig

# Programy probne: kilkanascie linii sprawdzajacych jedna rzecz na zywej
# maszynie (obsluga wyjatkow, ramka stosu). Sluzyly do zdiagnozowania konkretnej
# usterki i nie sa czescia portu.
/native/exctest*.cpp
/build/probe/

# Must come after "*.dat" above: the splash image the game loads at runtime.
# It is generated by build/make-splash.py, but that needs OpenGFX's grf to pull
# the real OpenTTD font glyphs out of, and we do not ship grf files - so the
# generated file itself has to be published for the port to build and run.
!/splash.dat
